Okay, let me try to figure out this problem. So, we have a polynomial $ P(x) $ with integer coefficients, and for every odd positive integer $ n $, $ nP(n) \equiv 1 \pmod{16} $. We need to find the minimum possible degree of such a polynomial. 

First, let me understand the condition. For every odd positive integer $ n $, when you multiply $ n $ by $ P(n) $ and take modulo 16, you get 1. So, $ nP(n) \equiv 1 \mod 16 $. Since $ n $ is odd, $ n $ is congruent to 1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11, 13, or 15 modulo 16. But since $ n $ is odd, it's in the set {1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11, 13, 15} mod 16. 

